From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 13:43:27 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 614/615] Slice anchor-first draft hidden states and use
 single-stage decode GEMM for Markov head

In DSparkSpeculator._sample_sequential and _sample_sequential_topk, when
sample_from_anchor is True (num_query_per_req == num_speculative_steps),
each request's query block has no leading anchor row and
head_hidden[:num_sample] is already contiguous in (req, step) order.
Slicing head_hidden[:num_sample] directly (and skipping
gather_hidden_rows_calls registration when sample_from_anchor is True)
eliminates a redundant gather_hidden_rows kernel launch and up to 800 KB
of HBM copy traffic per decode step.

In addition, when a BF16 vocabulary head tile covers the entire K
dimension in a single block (k <= block_K, such as DSpark's K=256
markov_w2 projection at block_M=16 and block_M=32), compile it with
bf16_gemm_decode (TL_DISABLE_WARP_SPECIALIZED=True) and num_stages=1.
A 1-iteration K loop has no second pipeline stage to overlap; disabling
warp specialization and 2-stage shared-memory double-buffering avoids
producer/consumer mbarrier overhead and halves shared memory per CTA
(40 KB vs 80 KB), doubling SM occupancy on GB10 from 1 CTA/SM to 2
CTAs/SM while preserving bit-exact FP32 accumulation across all tiles.
